hello youtube chess lovers my friends  this is gunjan here welcome to the third  episode of dirty checks tricks against  sicilian  in this lecture i'm going to show you  some of the amazing and wonderful tricks  against sicilian khan as well as  taimanov variation which arises after  the moon order e4 c5 knight to f3 and  now the moon e6  the main line goes as follows white  plays the moon d4  c captures d4 knight captures d4 and  here there is a parting of the vase  the sicilian taimano players will play  the move knight to c6 at this junction  where the sicilian khan player will play  the move a6 at this point  i found many similarities between these  two lines that's why i'm going to  concentrate the tricks where there's a  common setup used by both of the lines  which is  let's say when white respond with the  move knight to c3  black plays the move knight to c6  and any bishop moves for example bishop  to e2 then black plays the move queen to  c7  now first of all i'm going to show you  some of the sideline tricks in this  variations and then we are going to jump  on to the main line  so the first cylinder trick which i want  to show you which is a very cute one  and which helped me to win even against  a very high rated player  here there is a very tricky move  bishop to c4  which at first sight  doesn't make any sense because  this bishop can be easily hit by the  move b5  as the main line goes that black is  going to play queen to c7 anyways  there is a nice discovery on this bishop  when this knight is moved  so what exactly white is up to  well white has a very sneaky move at  this point that is  knight capture c6  now black can obviously capture with  either of the pawn and the game will  continue  but some of the taimano player will  tempt it to take with the queen because  it can easily hit the bishop and when  white defend this bishop with the move  let's say queen to e2  then they can get this initiative move  that is  b5 and it looks like  black is doing so good  but  there is a big problem  and the problem is  this one boom  so  white is winning the rook straight away  not to mention black cannot capture this  bishop because if black captures the  bishop then black will lose a queen  so this is a very cute trap exist in  sicilian taiwan or variation  the second sideline trap i want to show  you let's start with the move bishop to  f4  the idea is to stopping queen to c7 move  and what i found out is some of the  sicilian khan player will churn out the  move knight g to e7 at this point  and this especially happened in quick  games where black is looking forward for  the move d5  unfortunately this move has a big flaw  and  this can be exploited magnificently by  white's amazing peace sacrifice that is  knight d to b5  bam  so you can already see what is looking  for a checkmate threat on the d6 square  so obviously black has to accept this  piece sacrifice  and after knight captures b file the  threat remain as it is  so it looks like the only way to parry  this threat is to play the move d5  but this is exactly white once  now i want you to pause the video at  this point and find out a continuation  where white can actually win the black  queen  okay i hope you find this one that is  bishop to c1 attacking the queen so  queen has the only place to go that is  queen to d7  and now after knight to d6  the king has no place to go except black  has to give up the queen  so these are some of the nice sideline  tricks against  sicilian khan and taimanov variation  now let's see a main line trick in this  variations  now the main line goes as follows white  plays the move knight to c3 black plays  either of the move for example a6 or  queen c7  let's say in this game black plays the  move a6 first and after white reply with  the move bishop to e2  black plays the move queen to c7  here white will continue with the move  bishop to e3  black  continue with the move knight to f6  and after white castle on the king side  black plays this amazing move that is  bishop to b4  so idea is to capturing the knight  doubling the white pawn and then  capturing the e4 pawn  so what white is going to do about it  well here white has this amazing trick  starting with the move  knight to a4  at first side it looks completely stupid  because you are attacking that knight  and that knight run away straight away  and then  most of your opponent  think that oh  probably he missed that i can capture  the e4  well that is exactly why it wants  because the trick is set right here  now there's one thing i like to mention  that if black do not capture the pawn  and if black plays the normal move such  as  castle on the king side then after  knight capture c6  pawn captures  and f4  white initiative is running on the  king's side not to mention black cannot  capture this pawn at this junction  because after  queen to d4 black will lose a piece  so that's why  at this point  most of your opponent  definitely going to capture the pawn and  thinking that you have made a big  mistake  but see yourself  the following moves are very forceful  one and it almost give you a winning  advantage  so to illustrate this i like to show you  one of my own game where i kick start  with capturing the knight on the c6  now  black needs to be very careful over here  black cannot casually capture this  knight with the pawn for example if b  captures c6 then after queen to d4 black  is going to lose a piece  so the only move left is queen capture  c6  after this we are going to play the move  knight to b6 so another forcing move  rook has to move somewhere  my opponent responded here with the move  rook to b8  now the third  forcing move that is queen to d4 so  attacking this b4 bishop as well as the  g7 pawn so the only defense left is  bishop to f8  now the fourth attacking move that is  bishop to f3  attacking the e4 knight which cannot be  moved  so here black has the only two replies  left  if black plays the move f5 over here  then after bishop to h5  he has to move his king because the g7  pawn is spin  so let's say after king to d8 and rook a  to d1  it is the white who has  the pcs are placed so much aggressively  and look at the pathetic development of  the black pieces they are just sleeping  at the back side  so this cannot be good  so most of your opponent here will play  the move d5  and now we have the strongest move of  the game that is  c4  so what white is aiming for is to  destroying the pawn chain which is  supporting that knight  the only good move here for the black is  to play the move bishop to c5 which is  counter attacking the white queen  but once again the continuations are  very much forceful  so here white will continue with the  queen captures g7 attacking the rook  and after rook to f8  can you guessed it  yup is knight captures d5 boom  only two moves needs to be considered if  bishop captures e3 then the simplest is  knight to f6 jack  and after king to e7  we have the easiest move in the world  that is f captures e3 so not only  regaining a piece but that knight on e4  will be gone soon  so that is not good for the black and  if  black obviously going to play the move e  captures d5 which has happened in my  game  i captured the d5 pawn with the c pawn  attacking the queen  my opponent captured the d5 pawn  and now after bishop captures e4 queen  catches e4 and bishop captures c5  not to mention there's a threat of rook  to even  combining with the move queen captures  f8 and black cannot parry both of them  so my opponent resign at this point  i hope you enjoy this wonderful tricks  in the khan and taimano mainline  variations  feel free to like subscribe and comment  on my video and i'll meet you in my next  episode  bye

